From: Mark Harfouche <mark.harfouche@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 08:15:05 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Port the ResNet and MuNet pose nets to eager TensorFlow 2 /
 Keras

nnets/ builds its graphs with tf.compat.v1 and tf_slim: variable_scope,
arg_scope, slim.conv2d, slim.conv2d_transpose, slim.batch_norm and
tf_slim.nets.resnet_v1, all of it graph mode behind a Session.  This adds an
eager tf.keras implementation of the two nets defined in this directory --
PoseResnet (net_type=resnet_50/101/152) and PoseMultiNet (MuNet/DLCRNet and
the dual-fusion decoder) -- alongside the existing one, and a shim that moves
weights between the two in either direction.

The tf_slim implementation stays and stays the default.  Which one gets built
is decided by nnets/factory.resolve_engine: pose_cfg.yaml's tf_engine key
first, then the DLC_TF_ENGINE environment variable, then "tf1".  Nothing that
does not ask for "keras" changes behaviour.

What the Keras path is

  BaseKerasPoseNet (nnets/base.py) is a tf.keras.Model that also derives from
  BasePoseNet, so test(), inference(), add_inference_layers() and train() are
  the same code for both engines.  It adds

      extract_features(inputs, is_training=False) -> (net, end_points)
      get_net(inputs, is_training=False)          -> dict of heads
      slim_weight_map()  -> {tf_slim variable name: tf.Variable}

  SlimResNetV1 (nnets/resnet.py) is resnet_v1_{50,101,152} written out with
  slim's semantics.  tf.keras.applications.ResNet50 is a different network and
  cannot be used: Keras strides the 1x1 convolution of the first unit of each
  stage where slim strides the 3x3 of the last, Keras has no output_stride or
  dilation knob (DeepLabCut needs output_stride=16, Keras gives 28 for a
  200 px input), and Keras convolutions carry biases slim's do not, 318
  weights against 265.

  PoseMultiNetKeras (nnets/multi.py) reproduces the multi_fusion decoder, the
  upsampled_features stack for stride 8, 4 and 2, and the four-stage PAF
  decoder, plus the dual-fusion branch.

  nnets/checkpoint.py maps the two conventions onto each other:
  load_tf1_checkpoint, save_tf1_checkpoint (via tf.raw_ops.SaveV2, no Session),
  tf1_weight_values and compare_to_tf1_checkpoint.

  Backbones register with KerasBackboneFactory; any submodule that exposes
  slim_weights() is picked up by slim_weight_map(), so a backbone added later
  becomes checkpoint-compatible by implementing that one method.

Three tf_slim behaviours that are easy to get wrong, reproduced explicitly in
nnets/layers.py:

  slim.l2_regularizer(s) is s * sum(w**2) / 2 while keras.regularizers.l2(s)
  is s * sum(w**2), a silent factor of two on the weight decay.  The Keras
  layers use l2(0.5 * weight_decay).

  slim.conv2d and slim.conv2d_transpose create no bias when a normalizer_fn is
  given, which is why the five decoder_parallel_* convolutions have weights and
  BatchNorm variables but no biases.

  slim.batch_norm defaults to scale=False -- no gamma -- with decay 0.999 and
  epsilon 1e-3, while resnet_arg_scope() overrides those to scale=True, decay
  0.997 and epsilon 1e-5.  resnet_arg_scope() also overrides max_pool2d's
  padding from slim's VALID default to SAME, and it is called with no
  arguments, so the backbone's weight decay is 1e-4 whatever pose_cfg.yaml
  says.  conv2d_same's explicit pad plus VALID is reproduced too; padding=same
  is not the same thing at stride 2.

  tf.compat.v1.image.resize_images, which the multi_stage decoder uses for its
  two skip connections, is half_pixel_centers=False.  tf.image.resize is
  half_pixel_centers=True and is not a drop-in replacement; the port calls
  tf.raw_ops.ResizeBilinear directly.  The dual-fusion branch really does use
  the v2 resize, and that difference is preserved.

Batch norm follows the patched behaviour, not the stock one.  The resnet
backbone is built with is_training=False in both engines, so it always
normalises with its moving statistics and never updates them.  The MuNet
decoder threads is_training through, so at inference it uses the moving
averages and a prediction no longer depends on which other frames share its
batch.

Equivalence, measured against the tf_slim implementation with the real
ImageNet resnet_v1_50 and resnet_v1_101 weights restored, over 17
configurations covering single animal, multi-animal with a PAF head, an
identity channel, intermediate supervision, batch 4, resnet_50 and resnet_101,
MuNet at stride 8, 4 and 2, and dual fusion at stride 4:

  every variable maps 1:1, no shape mismatches and no unmapped Keras weight
      269 / 271 / 273 / 275 / 291 / 293 / 311 / 313 / 524 tensors
  max |delta| 3.2e-05 on features and 9.4e-06 on the heads
      (relative 2.4e-06, which is float32 accumulation order)
  28 further size/config combinations at 200x168, 201x169, 256x256, 99x101,
      350x480 and 161x129, same agreement
  predicted keypoints through predict.argmax_pose_predict agree to
      2.4e-05 px, and always to the same score-map cell
  the stock ImageNet resnet_v1_50.ckpt loads directly, 265 of its 269 tensors
      (the other three are the classifier head and mean_rgb, plus global_step)

The round trip works in both directions.  A tf_slim snapshot loads into the
Keras net, twelve tf.GradientTape steps train it -- 163 trainable variables
for resnet_50, 195 for MuNet, no None gradients -- save_tf1_checkpoint writes
it back out, and tf.compat.v1.train.Saver().restore reads it into the
unmodified tf_slim graph, matching to 1.6e-06.

The MuNet decoder's batch norm was checked against the patched tf_slim graph
directly.  With a batch of two where one image is held fixed:

  inference, keras   0.000000       inference, tf_slim   0.000000
  training,  keras   0.1794589      training,  tf_slim   0.1794586
  inference vs training, keras 0.2321826, tf_slim 0.2321830

Tests: tests/test_nnets_keras.py (49 tests, eager only) and
tests/test_nnets_keras_equivalence.py, which runs each engine in its own
subprocess because core/train.py calls tf.compat.v1.disable_eager_execution()
at import and the two cannot share a process.

Two upstream oddities are reproduced rather than fixed, because a snapshot
trained by the tf_slim engine has to keep loading:

  PoseMultiNet with multi_stage and no PAF head emits no part_pred at all --
  stage1_hm_out is built, so the variables exist, but out["part_pred"] is only
  assigned inside the partaffinityfield_predict branch -- so train() would
  raise KeyError.  MuNet is only usable multi-animal with a PAF head.

  The multi_stage decoder is unreachable in a stock project: PoseNetFactory
  routes to it only when stride < 8, and nothing writes such a value.
